592186243969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119796 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119796 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119796 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119796 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119796 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119796 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119796 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119796 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119796 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120002 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120002 #edb/ref 1045 "LU Factorization of 3x3 Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120002 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120002 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120002 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b4e56add-13ad-5fc4-ab0a-d5396c88286c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120190 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120190 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120190 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120190 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120190 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120190 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10430503-ea48-51a6-b4f8-6c1fb5c683b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120190 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1061 17360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1731663-b6e6-5bf1-a62a-a1b6ab66b554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1061 17361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02b00fc5-d232-5394-bd52-5c011f1db096" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1061 17362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "214a0b2c-29b3-58b2-a7c5-0300d4430cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120659 #edb/ref 1061 17364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dffaaec1-80eb-5582-bd94-3dde9c7b5f0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]$ is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1065 3178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5f8c6994-f448-5679-8d2e-5591b86c2a97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241194 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241194 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241194 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0103ed51-38eb-56e1-b5b6-506ac5114dcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241507 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241507 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241507 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "25f20815-d3c9-5266-a9ea-efd0b9f40481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241538 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241538 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241538 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28f58628-e349-51a5-9381-2a6c3f3e63d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241553 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241553 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241553 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a100052-8c46-523f-bb66-e7fb58725152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241655 #edb/r